<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Hardened-runtime exceptions. These only take effect on a signed build (the current releases
       are unsigned), and the file is also inherited by every nested binary (entitlementsInherit).

       Electron (V8) needs JIT, and allow-jit is all it needs: @electron/osx-sign's own default
       entitlements grant nothing else from the cs.* family. Deliberately NOT granted:
         - allow-unsigned-executable-memory: writable+executable memory without MAP_JIT, a wider
           exception that current Electron does not require;
         - allow-dyld-environment-variables: would let DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES and friends inject code
           into the app. Neither the PyInstaller backend nor the bundled PostgreSQL relies on DYLD_*
           variables (both locate their libraries by @rpath / @loader_path).
       If a signed build ever shows the frozen Python backend failing on executable memory (ctypes /
       cffi callbacks), re-add allow-unsigned-executable-memory rather than disabling the runtime. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- Kept: the bundled PostgreSQL and the PyInstaller backend are third-party prebuilt binaries
       that load their own dylibs / extension modules. Library validation would require every one
       of those to carry the app's Team ID; until that is verified on a real signed build, the
       child processes only launch reliably with it disabled. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
